<body>

<h1>AI Internal Linking</h1>
<p class="sub">Every scoring formula the plugin uses to decide that page A should link to page B, and with which anchor. Version 0.15.1.</p>

<h2>How a suggestion is produced</h2>

<p>For each published page the plugin runs three suggestion engines in a fixed order. Each one consumes the same link budget, so an earlier engine wins when both would propose a link to the same target. Every engine, without exception, must find an anchor phrase that already exists in the source page's body text. If no such phrase exists, no suggestion is created.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Order</th><th>Engine</th><th>Evidence it uses</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pass 1</td><td>GSC keyword</td><td>Search Console: a query the target already ranks for</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pass 2</td><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>A chat model reading the page and a shortlist</td><td>Your API key</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pass 3</td><td>Related Content</td><td>Local TF-IDF vocabulary overlap</td><td>Free</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="key"><strong>The one rule that governs everything:</strong> the plugin links text that is already on the page. It never writes new sentences, never invents an anchor, and never inserts anything without your approval.</div>

<h2>1. AI suggestion formula</h2>

<p>Runs only when "AI link suggestions" is enabled in Settings and a chat provider key is configured. Works with any chat model, including Claude, OpenAI, Gemini, Groq, xAI, DeepSeek, OpenRouter, Perplexity, and local models.</p>

<h3>What the model is allowed to see</h3>

<p>The model never browses your site and never chooses freely from all pages. It receives a closed shortlist produced by the free engine:</p>

<div class="formula">candidate_pool = top 18 TF-IDF candidates for this page
                 minus the page itself
                 minus every target already linked or already suggested

sent_to_model  = first 15 of that pool     (MAX_CANDIDATES)
page_excerpt   = first N words of the body  (Setup and Dashboard setting,
                 default 1,000 words, maximum 2,500)
max_tokens     = 800
temperature    = 0</div>

<div class="note"><strong>The AI engine does not read the whole page.</strong> It reads the first N words, where N is the "Words per page sent to the AI" setting on Setup and Dashboard. At the default of 1,000 words, a 3,000 word article is about one third visible, so AI suggestions cluster near the opening of long posts. Raise the setting to widen that window; the token bill scales almost linearly with it, because every post in a scan is one request.<br /><br />This limit applies to the AI engine ONLY. GSC keyword evidence and Related Content always read the entire page, and a proposed anchor is always verified against the full text rather than the excerpt.</div>

<h3>How a returned link is scored</h3>

<div class="formula">relevance = clamp(model_confidence, 0, 1)
            default 0.7 when the model returns no confidence value</div>

<h3>How the link is actually chosen, step by step</h3>

<p>This is the part that decides everything, and it is more constrained than "the AI picks a link" suggests. Two facts do most of the work:</p>

<div class="key"><strong>The model never sees a URL, and never writes one.</strong> It is shown a numbered list of page titles and replies with a number. The address is looked up afterwards, by the plugin, from its own index.<br /><br /><strong>The wording that gets saved is your text, not the model's text.</strong> Whatever phrase the model quotes is searched for in your page, and the version found in your page is the one stored, including your capitalisation.</div>

<p><strong>What the model receives.</strong> Three things, and nothing else:</p>

<div class="formula">ARTICLE TITLE: How to Build a Content Calendar

ARTICLE TEXT:
...planning ahead means your content marketing strategy stays
consistent across the quarter...          (the first N words)

CANDIDATE PAGES:
1. Content Marketing Strategy: A Complete Guide
2. Social Media Scheduling Tools
3. Editorial Workflow for Small Teams
   ... up to 15</div>

<p>No addresses, no page IDs, no database values. Just your title, part of your text, and a numbered list of titles.</p>

<p><strong>What the model must reply.</strong> JSON in one fixed shape, nothing else:</p>

<div class="formula">{"links":[
  {"n": 1,
   "anchor": "content marketing strategy",
   "reason": "the article is about planning a strategy",
   "confidence": 0.86}
]}</div>

<p><strong>What the plugin then does with that reply.</strong> Every step can reject it:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Is <code>n</code> between 1 and the number of candidates shown?</td><td>Discarded. A number outside the list is meaningless</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Look up candidate 1. That is a real page: its ID and its address come from the plugin's own index</td><td>Cannot fail. The model had no say in the address</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Is it a different page from the one being scanned, and not already used in this batch?</td><td>Discarded</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Search your article for the quoted phrase, whole word, ignoring capitals</td><td>Discarded. This is the step that kills invented wording</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Keep the text <em>as it appears in your article</em>, plus about 90 characters either side as context</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Take the model's confidence as the relevance score, clamped to 0 and 1</td><td>0.7 assumed if absent</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>The result stored for review:</strong></p>

<div class="formula">target page  = candidate 1        -> post 482
target URL   = from the plugin's index, never from the model
anchor text  = "Content Marketing Strategy"
               (your capitalisation, because that is what was
                found in your article, not the lowercase the
                model typed)
context      = "...planning ahead means your Content Marketing
                Strategy stays consistent across the quarter..."
relevance    = 0.86</div>

<p><strong>Why it is built this way.</strong> A model asked to produce a URL will eventually produce a plausible one that does not exist. A model asked to produce anchor text will eventually produce a tidier phrasing than the one on the page. Neither failure is possible here: the address is never in the model's hands, and the wording is re-found in your own text before anything is saved. The model's job is narrow on purpose, choose a number and quote a phrase, and both answers are checked against facts the plugin already holds.</p>

<h3>The three checks that make fabrication impossible</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Check</th><th>Rule</th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>Real target</td><td>The chosen page must be one of the candidates that was sent</td><td>Pick discarded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Real anchor</td><td>The proposed anchor must appear word for word in the body text</td><td>Pick discarded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Not a heading</td><td>Headings and the page title are stripped before the model ever sees the text</td><td>Cannot be chosen</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="key">A model that invents a page, invents a phrase, or paraphrases your wording produces nothing. The suggestion is dropped silently and the free engines fill the gap.</div>

<h3>What one page costs</h3>

<div class="formula">per page:  about 2,400 input tokens  (1,500 body + 750 candidates + 200 system)
           up to    800 output tokens
           exactly    1 request

a 300 page site: roughly 720,000 input and up to 240,000 output tokens per scan</div>

<h2>2. GSC keyword evidence formula</h2>

<p>The strongest signal available, because it is grounded in what people actually search for rather than in what your pages resemble. The model is: page B already ranks for query Q, page A says Q in its body, page A does not link to B, therefore link it using Q as the anchor. Runs first and takes budget before the other engines.</p>

<h3>Step 1, click through rate by position</h3>

<p>Opportunity is expressed in clicks rather than impressions, which needs an estimate of how often a result at a given position is clicked.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Position</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr>
<tr><td>CTR</td><td>0.280</td><td>0.150</td><td>0.110</td><td>0.080</td><td>0.060</td><td>0.048</td><td>0.039</td><td>0.032</td><td>0.028</td><td>0.025</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="formula">positions 11 to 20:  ctr = max(0.008, 0.025 - (position - 10) * 0.0017)
position 21 and up:  ctr = 0.005</div>

<h3>Step 2, opportunity score</h3>

<div class="formula">gain        = ctr(position 3) - ctr(current position), floored at 0
reach       = 1.0            when position is 20 or better
            = 20 / position  when position is worse than 20

opportunity = impressions * gain * reach</div>

<p>In words: the extra clicks this keyword could realistically win if the page reached the top three, discounted by how plausible that jump is. Positions 1, 2 and 3 score exactly zero, because a page already at the top has nothing to gain from another internal link. The score peaks around position 20 and falls away below it, since one internal link will not lift page five into the top three.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Position</th><th>1</th><th>3</th><th>5</th><th>10</th><th>15</th><th>20</th><th>40</th><th>99</th></tr>
<tr><td>Score per 1,000 impressions</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>50.0</td><td>85.0</td><td>93.5</td><td>102.0</td><td>52.5</td><td>21.2</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>Changed in 0.13.0.</strong> The previous formula was <code>impressions * (21 - min(20, position)) / 20</code>, which peaked at position 1. Keywords you already ranked first for scored highest and consumed the link budget, which is backwards, and it fought the striking distance bonus it was paired with. Existing keyword rows keep their old scores until you re-fetch from Search Console or re-import your CSV.</div>

<h3>Step 3, striking distance</h3>

<div class="formula">is_striking = position is between 5 and 20 inclusive</div>

<p>This is the band where one more internal link measurably moves a ranking. High enough that the page is already credible to the search engine, low enough that there is real room to climb.</p>

<h3>Step 4, relevance</h3>

<div class="formula">relevance = min(0.98,  0.50                                          base
                     + 0.20  if is_striking
                     + 0.28 * min(1, opportunity / best_opportunity_in_pool))</div>

<p>The base of 0.50 sits deliberately above what vocabulary overlap normally produces, so a keyword backed suggestion outranks a merely similar page. The pool is the top 500 keywords by opportunity.</p>

<h3>How the link is actually chosen, step by step</h3>

<div class="key"><strong>Neither half of this link is chosen by the plugin.</strong> Google decides the destination, because Search Console already knows which of your pages answers that query. The people searching decide the wording, because the anchor is the phrase they actually typed. The plugin's only job is to notice where you forgot to link.</div>

<p><strong>What Search Console hands over.</strong> One row per query and landing page:</p>

<div class="formula">query        content marketing strategy
page         /guides/content-marketing-strategy/
impressions  1,200
position     7</div>

<p><strong>Turning that into a link, step by step:</strong></p>

<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Resolve the landing page address to a post on this site</td><td>The row is dropped. A query that ranks a page the plugin cannot identify is unusable</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><strong>The target is now decided.</strong> That post is the destination, chosen by Google's index, not by any scoring the plugin performs</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><strong>The wording is now decided.</strong> The query string itself is the candidate anchor</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Is the query between 2 and 4 words?</td><td>Skipped. A one word anchor is not descriptive, a nine word one is a sentence</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Does the page being scanned contain that exact query, as whole words?</td><td>Skipped. Nothing to link</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Does that page already link to the target?</td><td>Skipped. It is not an unlinked mention if it is already linked</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Would this be the fourth identical anchor pointing at that target?</td><td>Skipped, to avoid an over-optimised pattern</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Keep the text <em>as it appears in the page being scanned</em>, plus context either side</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>The result stored for review:</strong></p>

<div class="formula">target page  = the post that ranks for the query   -> post 482
target URL   = from the plugin's index
anchor text  = "Content Marketing Strategy"
               (your capitalisation, taken from the page being
                scanned, not the lowercase Google reports)
relevance    = 0.50 base + 0.20 striking + opportunity share</div>

<p>Only the highest value 500 keywords are considered per scan, ordered by opportunity, and each target page receives at most one keyword suggestion per source page: the best keyword wins, because the pool is already sorted.</p>

<h3>Step 5, the over optimisation guard</h3>

<div class="formula">refuse the suggestion when
  (existing links with this exact anchor to this target)
+ (open suggestions with this exact anchor to this target)  >=  3</div>

<p>Keyword anchors are exact match by nature, which is precisely the pattern that looks manipulative at scale. The cap is adjustable through the <code>ailinking_max_exact_anchors_per_target</code> filter.</p>

<h2>3. Anchor selection formula</h2>

<div class="note"><strong>Correction to an earlier version of this document.</strong> It said the anchor is drawn from the target page's title for every engine. That is true of one engine only. All three share the same whole-word matching step, but each supplies its own candidate phrase.</div>

<table>
<tr><th>Engine</th><th>Where the candidate anchor phrase comes from</th></tr>
<tr><td>GSC keyword</td><td>The ranking query itself, taken from Search Console. The title is not involved.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>A phrase the model picks out of the article text. The title is not involved.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Related Content</td><td>Word sequences built from the TARGET page's title. This is the title based engine.</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Why the title, for that engine.</strong> The free engine knows two pages are related, but nothing about what the link should be called. A page title is a human written summary of what the destination is about, so phrases taken from it describe where the link goes. That is what good anchor text does, for a reader and for a search engine alike. The alternative, grabbing any related-looking phrase from the source, produces anchors that describe nothing.</p>

<p><strong>The cost of that choice.</strong> If no phrase from the target's title appears in the source page, the Related Content engine produces nothing for that pair, however well matched the two pages are. This is the single biggest reason a page you expect to be linked is not. The other two engines are not bound by it, which is a large part of why they run first.</p>

<p>The steps below are the Related Content path. The other engines skip to step 6 with their own phrase.</p>

<div class="formula">1. Split the target title on  :  ;  ,  |  ( )  [ ]  /  and dashes
   so a phrase never bridges a punctuation boundary.

2. Build every word sequence inside each segment,
   from max_anchor_words (default 4) down to min_anchor_words (default 2).

3. Reject a phrase when:
     the first or last word is a stopword
     any word is a pure number
     any word is part, parts, vol, volume, chapter, edition, section, no
     the phrase is shorter than 6 characters

4. Order: most words first, then longest string. Keep the best 60.

5. Optional single word fallback, only when min_anchor_words is 1:
     6 or more letters, not a stopword,
     not guide, review, tips, best, introduction, overview, about, guidelines

6. Search the body for each phrase in order, whole word, case insensitive:
     (?&lt;![letter or digit]) phrase (?![letter or digit])

7. First match wins. No match at all means no suggestion.</div>

<p>The ordering is the point: the longest and most specific phrase from the title that genuinely appears in your text becomes the anchor, so anchors describe their destination rather than being generic.</p>

<h3>Never eligible as anchor text</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Excluded</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
<tr><td>Anything in H1 to H6</td><td>Headings are stripped from the indexed text entirely</td></tr>
<tr><td>The page title</td><td>Removed from the body corpus before indexing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Text already inside a link</td><td>Never nested</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code blocks and tag attributes</td><td>Would corrupt the markup</td></tr>
<tr><td>Text in another language</td><td>Cross language links are never proposed</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>4. Naturalness formula</h2>

<p>Measures whether the link reads like something a writer would have typed, rather than something bolted on for a search engine.</p>

<div class="formula">naturalness = 0.45
            + 0.30 * relevance
            + 0.15  if the anchor contains a space (multi word)
            + 0.10  if the anchor is 8 to 60 characters
            - 0.20  if the anchor is shorter than 5 characters
            - 0.15  if the anchor is longer than 70 characters
            clamped to the range 0 to 1</div>

<h2>5. Final confidence formula</h2>

<div class="formula">confidence = 0.6 * relevance + 0.4 * naturalness</div>

<div class="note">This composite is what the Suggestions tab displays in the column labelled "Relevance". Relevance answers "should these two pages be connected", naturalness answers "does this specific placement read well", and the link is worth making only when both hold.</div>

<h2>6. Budget and density formulas</h2>

<div class="formula">max_allowed = max(2, ceil(word_count / 1000 * max_links_per_1000))   default 5 per 1,000 words
capacity    = max_allowed - links already in the content
limit       = min(max_suggestions_post, capacity)                   default cap 8

capacity of 0 or less  ->  the page is skipped entirely</div>

<p>A target is also skipped when the source already links to it, or when a suggestion for that exact pair already exists in any state, including rejected. The same pair is never proposed twice. Candidates scoring below <code>min_relevance</code> (default 0.08) are discarded.</p>

<h2>7. Token and cost formulas</h2>

<div class="formula">cost_in_cents = (tokens_in * rate_in + tokens_out * rate_out) / 1,000,000
                where the rates are cents per million tokens for the model

recorded cost   = the exact value above, stored at 6 decimal places
spend cap check = ceil(the value above), so the cap can never under enforce
pre-flight size = characters / 4, used to estimate before a call is made</div>

<div class="note"><strong>Fixed in 0.13.0.</strong> Every call used to be rounded up to a whole cent before being recorded. A typical 2,400 token request costing about 0.04 cents was logged as a full cent, over twenty times high, and a few hundred calls could trip the monthly spend cap at a small fraction of real spend. Costs are now recorded exactly.</div>

<h2>8. Related Content formula (TF-IDF)</h2>

<p>The always available baseline. No keys, no external calls, no cost. It runs last and fills whatever budget the other engines left. This is also the engine that builds the candidate shortlist the AI pass depends on, so it is doing work even when AI suggestions are enabled.</p>

<h3>Step 1, building the vocabulary</h3>

<div class="formula">Strip H1 to H6 and the page title from the body.
Lowercase, then take every token matching:  letter followed by 2 or more letters, digits, apostrophes or hyphens
Drop stopwords and anything shorter than 3 characters.
Count each term. Keep the 300 most frequent per page.</div>

<h3>Step 2, term weighting</h3>

<div class="formula">N      = number of indexed pages
df(t)  = number of pages containing term t

Discard t when df(t) is less than 1        (too rare to matter)
Discard t when df(t) is more than 0.4 * N  (too common to tell pages apart)

idf(t) = ln(1 + N / df(t))</div>

<h3>Step 3, scoring a candidate page</h3>

<div class="formula">source_terms = the source page's 40 most frequent terms

source_weight = sum over t of  idf(t) * source_tf(t)

raw(C)        = sum over t of  idf(t) * min(source_tf(t), candidate_tf(t))

Require at least 2 shared significant terms, otherwise discard C.

relevance(C)  = min(1, raw(C) / source_weight)</div>

<h3>How the link is actually chosen, step by step</h3>

<div class="key"><strong>This engine picks the destination first and works out the wording afterwards</strong>, which is the opposite order to the other two. That ordering has a consequence worth knowing: a perfect subject match still produces nothing if no phrase from the target's title happens to appear in your text.</div>

<p><strong>Step by step:</strong></p>

<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Score every other page by vocabulary overlap and sort, best first</td><td>No shared vocabulary anywhere means no suggestions for this page</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Walk that list in order. Skip the page itself, anything already linked, and anything already suggested or rejected before</td><td>Move to the next candidate</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Is the score at least 0.08?</td><td>Move on. Everything below is coincidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td><strong>The target is now provisional.</strong> Take that page's <em>title</em> and cut it at punctuation, then build every word group from 4 words down to 2</td><td>A title with no usable group is abandoned</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Throw away groups that begin or end on a filler word, contain a bare number, or contain words like "part" or "chapter"</td><td>Those groups are dropped</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Sort what is left longest first, then search your page for each in turn</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>First group found in your page wins and becomes the anchor</td><td><strong>If none of them appears, the whole pairing is abandoned</strong> and the next candidate is tried, however high the score was</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Keep the text as it appears in your page, plus context either side</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Why longest first.</strong> "content marketing strategy" describes its destination; "strategy" does not. Trying the longest groups first means the most specific wording available is always preferred.</p>

<p><strong>The result stored for review:</strong></p>

<div class="formula">target page  = the best scoring candidate whose title
               could be quoted from your text     -> post 482
target URL   = from the plugin's index
anchor text  = "Content Marketing Strategy"
               (a word group from the TARGET page's title,
                found in YOUR page)
relevance    = the vocabulary overlap score       -> 0.42</div>

<div class="note"><strong>The most common reason an expected link is missing.</strong> Step 7. Two pages can be plainly about the same subject and still produce nothing, because your text never happens to contain a phrase from the other page's title. The other two engines are not bound by this, which is a large part of why they run first.</div>

<h3>Why it is shaped this way</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Choice</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>min(source_tf, candidate_tf)</code></td><td>A page repeating a term 500 times gets credit only up to how often the source uses it, so keyword stuffed pages cannot dominate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dividing by <code>source_weight</code></td><td>Turns the score into "how much of this page's meaningful vocabulary does the candidate cover", which lands naturally between 0 and 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>The <code>0.4 * N</code> ceiling</td><td>Words appearing on nearly every page carry no signal and are dropped automatically, whatever language they are in</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two shared terms minimum</td><td>A single coincidental word cannot create a link</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="key">Nothing here knows your subject matter. The vocabulary is whatever your pages actually contain, and the weights are computed from your own site at scan time. The same formulas produce recipe links on a recipe site and legal links on a law firm site, with no configuration.</div>

<h3>Known limits</h3>

<p>Relevance here is lexical, not semantic. Two pages sharing rare vocabulary score highly even when they argue different things, and two pages making the same point in different words score nothing. Closing that gap is exactly what the AI suggestion pass is for. The stopword list is English only, so on other languages the document frequency ceiling in step 2 does most of the filtering. The list is replaceable through the <code>ailinking_stopwords</code> filter.</p>

<h2>Worked example</h2>

<p>Source page mentions the phrase "content marketing strategy". Target page is titled "Content Marketing Strategy: A Complete Guide".</p>

<div class="formula">1. Title splits at the colon, giving the segment "Content Marketing Strategy".
2. The 3 word phrase is built, passes the noise checks, and is found in the body.
   anchor = "Content Marketing Strategy"    (26 characters, multi word)

3. Suppose the TF-IDF relevance came out at 0.42.

4. naturalness = 0.45 + (0.30 * 0.42) = 0.576
                + 0.15  multi word        = 0.726
                + 0.10  length 8 to 60    = 0.826

5. confidence = (0.6 * 0.42) + (0.4 * 0.826)
              = 0.252 + 0.3304
              = 0.582</div>

<p>The suggestion enters the review inbox showing relevance 0.42, naturalness 0.83 and an overall 0.58, with the surrounding sentence quoted so the placement can be judged before anything is approved.</p>

